solution.p:
Differentiating the left side needs both chain rule and product rule:
\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{dx} e^{x^5 y} \amp = e^{x^5 y} \cdot \left( \frac{d}{dx} x^5 y\right)\\
\amp = e^{x^5 y} \left({5x^{4}} + x^5 \frac{dy}{dx}\right)
\end{aligned}

Solving for \displaystyle{\frac{dy}{dx}} gives
\begin{aligned}
{5x^{4}}e^{x^5 y} + x^5e^{x^5 y} \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = 5+10 \frac{dy}{dx}\\
\left(x^5e^{x^5 y} - 10\right) \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = 5 - {5x^{4}}e^{x^5 y}\\
\frac{dy}{dx} \amp = \frac{5 - {5x^{4}}e^{x^5 y}}{x^5e^{x^5 y} - 10}
\end{aligned}
